WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.890
Violet light scatters in the air even more than blue does, so by rights the sky ought to be violet.

2
00:00:05.890 --> 00:00:10.820
The colour we see when we look up is a compromise between physics and our own eyes.

3
00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:16.230
On Mars, that compromise is turned on its head, and there the sunset is blue.

4
00:00:24.430 --> 00:00:30.160
When you look up at a clear midday sky, the colour you see is really the answer to an enormous question.

5
00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:34.450
For centuries, people simply accepted this blue as it was.

6
00:00:34.450 --> 00:00:41.780
Some thought it was the colour of the air itself; others believed it was a mixture of the darkness of space and the light of the sun.

7
00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:48.790
In his notebooks, Leonardo da Vinci wrote that blue arises from fine particles lit up against a dark background.

8
00:00:48.790 --> 00:00:53.400
His intuition was remarkably good, but he had no experiment to back it up.

9
00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:59.770
To truly understand why the sky is blue, someone first had to recreate it inside a glass tube.

10
00:00:59.770 --> 00:01:03.580
That someone turned out to be the Irish physicist John Tyndall.

11
00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:09.150
In the eighteen sixties, the Royal Institution in London was Tyndall's laboratory.

12
00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:13.280
There he studied how light passes through gases and vapours.

13
00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:20.610
An experiment he carried out in eighteen sixty-nine produced, in his own words, a kind of artificial sky.

14
00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:29.540
He filled a long, horizontal glass tube with a very dilute organic vapour and, using a lens, sent the beam of a powerful lamp in through one end.

15
00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:33.350
At first the tube was empty, invisible.

16
00:01:33.350 --> 00:01:41.000
But as the light interacted with the vapour, a chemical reaction triggered by the light began to form extraordinarily tiny particles.

17
00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:48.490
And at that moment, inside the tube, right where the beam passed through, a faint but unmistakable blue cloud appeared.

18
00:01:48.490 --> 00:01:52.140
What truly excited Tyndall was not just the colour.

19
00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:59.790
When he looked at this blue cloud from the side, at a right angle to the beam, he noticed that the light coming from it was polarised.

20
00:01:59.790 --> 00:02:06.800
In other words, the light waves were not vibrating randomly in every direction, but within a particular plane.

21
00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:12.370
This detail mattered, because the real sky was known to do exactly the same thing.

22
00:02:12.370 --> 00:02:22.100
The French physicist François Arago had shown in eighteen oh-nine that skylight is polarised, and that the effect is strongest at points ninety degrees away from the sun.

23
00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:26.710
Tyndall's tube mimicked the sky both in its colour and in its polarisation.

24
00:02:26.710 --> 00:02:35.800
The conclusion he drew was this: the sky is blue because the atmosphere contains particles, far smaller than the wavelength of light, that scatter it.

25
00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:41.050
Tyndall thought these particles might be fine dust and water droplets suspended in the air.

26
00:02:41.050 --> 00:02:47.260
Tyndall was an experimenter; he had made the observation, but he had not built the mathematics behind it.

27
00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:54.430
That gap was filled by a young English aristocrat: John William Strutt, known to the scientific world as Lord Rayleigh.

28
00:02:54.430 --> 00:03:05.120
In papers published in eighteen seventy-one, Rayleigh asked a simple but extraordinarily powerful question: how does an obstacle much smaller than the wavelength of light respond to a light wave?

29
00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:10.530
To understand the answer, it is enough to think of light as a wave of electric and magnetic fields.

30
00:03:10.530 --> 00:03:17.060
As this wave passes over a tiny particle, it shakes the electric charges inside the particle back and forth.

31
00:03:17.060 --> 00:03:22.630
And those shaking charges, like tiny antennas, send out new light in every direction.

32
00:03:22.630 --> 00:03:28.520
That is what scattering is: part of the incoming light is knocked off its path and spread all around.

33
00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:35.050
Through an elegant line of reasoning known as dimensional analysis, followed by more detailed calculations,

34
00:03:35.050 --> 00:03:41.580
Rayleigh showed that the intensity of the scattered light is inversely proportional to the fourth power of the wavelength.

35
00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:48.750
Halve the wavelength, and the scattering does not double; it grows by two to the fourth power, that is, sixteen times.

36
00:03:48.750 --> 00:03:52.720
This fourth power is the hidden rule that sets the colour of the sky.

37
00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:59.570
In visible light, red has a wavelength of around seven hundred nanometres, and blue around four hundred and fifty.

38
00:03:59.570 --> 00:04:07.060
Plug those numbers into Rayleigh's formula, and you find that blue light is scattered roughly six times more strongly than red.

39
00:04:07.060 --> 00:04:15.350
When white light from the sun enters the air, its short-wavelength components are flung in every direction and reach your eyes from every corner of the sky.

40
00:04:15.350 --> 00:04:20.440
The long wavelengths, meanwhile, largely carry straight on along their path.

41
00:04:20.440 --> 00:04:27.610
When you look not at the sun itself but at a point far from it, what you see is this scattered light, shifted towards blue.

42
00:04:27.610 --> 00:04:30.940
The formula also explained polarisation.

43
00:04:30.940 --> 00:04:38.110
A shaking charge cannot emit light along the direction of its own oscillation; it emits most strongly at right angles to it.

44
00:04:38.110 --> 00:04:43.840
That is why light coming from the band of sky at a right angle to the sun is strongly polarised.

45
00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:49.090
Today we know that honeybees and some migratory animals use this pattern like a compass.

46
00:04:49.090 --> 00:04:55.300
A single physical principle of Rayleigh's explained both the colour and this invisible pattern at the same time.

47
00:04:55.300 --> 00:04:58.470
But the story did not end there.

48
00:04:58.470 --> 00:05:02.280
Because two troubling questions still stood in the way.

49
00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:07.690
The first: was the thing scattering this light really dust and droplets?

50
00:05:07.690 --> 00:05:16.300
The second, and even more vexing: if the formula was so clear, and shorter wavelengths scatter more strongly, why was the sky blue and not violet?

51
00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:21.550
The answer to the first question came from a place that seemed strange at first glance.

52
00:05:21.550 --> 00:05:27.440
When Rayleigh returned to the subject in eighteen ninety-nine, he argued that dust was not needed at all.

53
00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:37.170
The molecules of the air itself, nitrogen and oxygen, were thousands of times smaller than the wavelength of light, and they too could act on their own like tiny antennas.

54
00:05:37.170 --> 00:05:45.140
Rayleigh pointed out that on mountain summits, in air almost entirely free of dust, the sky stayed blue, and was even a deeper blue.

55
00:05:45.140 --> 00:05:48.790
The source of the scattering had to be the air itself.

56
00:05:48.790 --> 00:05:54.680
But there was a trap hidden inside this idea, and it took physicists a decade to notice it.

57
00:05:54.680 --> 00:05:58.970
Imagine a perfectly uniform, completely homogeneous medium.

58
00:05:58.970 --> 00:06:02.460
A flawless block of glass, for example.

59
00:06:02.460 --> 00:06:07.870
Inside the glass there are countless atoms too, and each one oscillates with the incoming light.

60
00:06:07.870 --> 00:06:11.360
Yet glass does not scatter light to the sides.

61
00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:14.370
It is transparent.

62
00:06:14.370 --> 00:06:16.420
Why?

63
00:06:16.420 --> 00:06:25.030
Because when atoms are evenly arranged, the wave each tiny antenna sends sideways is met by its neighbor's wave in exactly the opposite phase.

64
00:06:25.030 --> 00:06:27.400
The waves cancel each other out.

65
00:06:27.400 --> 00:06:30.730
Only the light travelling forward survives.

66
00:06:30.730 --> 00:06:33.740
Perfect order kills scattering.

67
00:06:33.740 --> 00:06:38.350
So why does air glow blue instead of being as transparent as glass?

68
00:06:38.350 --> 00:06:44.880
This is where the Polish physicist Marian Smoluchowski, and after him Albert Einstein, enter the story.

69
00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:54.930
Smoluchowski in nineteen oh-eight, and Einstein in a famous paper published in nineteen ten, showed that the answer lay not in the molecules themselves but in their disorder.

70
00:06:54.930 --> 00:06:58.740
Gas molecules move about randomly and without pause.

71
00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:05.430
So when you divide the air into small volumes, the number of molecules in each one is never exactly the same.

72
00:07:05.430 --> 00:07:11.000
At any moment one corner holds a few molecules more than average, and another a few fewer.

73
00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:14.490
The density is constantly fluctuating.

74
00:07:14.490 --> 00:07:20.700
And it is these small, random fluctuations that keep the scattered waves from cancelling each other out completely.

75
00:07:20.700 --> 00:07:24.350
Einstein's real goal was not actually the sky.

76
00:07:24.350 --> 00:07:28.800
He was trying to explain a phenomenon called critical opalescence.

77
00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:39.170
When a liquid approaches the special critical point between boiling and becoming a gas, it suddenly turns cloudy, like milk, because its density fluctuations grow enormous.

78
00:07:39.170 --> 00:07:43.300
Einstein calculated how much light these fluctuations would scatter.

79
00:07:43.300 --> 00:07:49.030
Then, when he applied his formula to an ordinary gas, something beautiful happened.

80
00:07:49.030 --> 00:07:53.000
Rayleigh's fourth-power law reappeared all by itself.

81
00:07:53.000 --> 00:08:03.850
Because the positions of molecules in an ideal gas are independent of one another, the calculation based on fluctuations and the calculation based on individual molecules arrived at the same result.

82
00:08:03.850 --> 00:08:06.380
But now we knew why it was true.

83
00:08:06.380 --> 00:08:12.910
What makes the sky blue is the grainy, discrete nature of air, the fact that it is made of atoms.

84
00:08:12.910 --> 00:08:17.200
If air were a continuous, smooth fluid, the sky would be black.

85
00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:21.170
This result had a dramatic side effect.

86
00:08:21.170 --> 00:08:25.940
The amount of scattering depended on the number of molecules per unit volume.

87
00:08:25.940 --> 00:08:34.230
That meant if you measured the brightness of the sky carefully, you could calculate the number of molecules, in other words, Avogadro's number.

88
00:08:34.230 --> 00:08:39.960
Rayleigh had tried this roughly, and with Einstein's calculation the method was put on solid ground.

89
00:08:39.960 --> 00:08:48.090
At a time when people were still arguing over whether atoms really existed, the blue above our heads became a silent witness to their existence.

90
00:08:48.090 --> 00:08:53.180
Now let us return to the second, more stubborn question: the missing violet.

91
00:08:53.180 --> 00:08:55.710
Rayleigh's law is merciless.

92
00:08:55.710 --> 00:09:05.120
Violet light has a wavelength of around four hundred nanometers, and it scatters about one point six times more strongly than blue at four hundred fifty nanometers.

93
00:09:05.120 --> 00:09:08.450
On paper, the sky should be painted violet.

94
00:09:08.450 --> 00:09:10.500
So why isn't it?

95
00:09:10.500 --> 00:09:14.630
The answer lies not in a single cause but in a chain of three links.

96
00:09:14.630 --> 00:09:17.000
The first link is the Sun itself.

97
00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:21.130
Sunlight does not contain all colors in equal amounts.

98
00:09:21.130 --> 00:09:29.420
The solar spectrum measured outside the atmosphere reaches its peak somewhere between green and blue, and falls off noticeably toward violet.

99
00:09:29.420 --> 00:09:38.990
Elements such as calcium and iron in the Sun's outer layers create many narrow dark lines at the violet end of the spectrum, swallowing part of that light from the very start.

100
00:09:38.990 --> 00:09:42.960
So there is less violet light to scatter than blue.

101
00:09:42.960 --> 00:09:47.250
The second link is that what scattering produces is not a pure color.

102
00:09:47.250 --> 00:09:52.020
Rayleigh scattering does not pick out a single wavelength and throw away the rest.

103
00:09:52.020 --> 00:09:56.950
It scatters the whole spectrum, just the short wavelengths more strongly.

104
00:09:56.950 --> 00:10:05.240
The light that reaches your eye from the sky is a broad mixture containing every color from violet to red, weighted more and more toward the short wavelengths.

105
00:10:05.240 --> 00:10:10.170
Within this mixture there is violet, but also green, and yellow.

106
00:10:10.170 --> 00:10:14.460
The third, and perhaps most decisive, link is your eye.

107
00:10:14.460 --> 00:10:21.630
In our retina there are three kinds of cone cells that let us see color: those sensitive to short, medium and long wavelengths.

108
00:10:21.630 --> 00:10:27.360
That broad mixture coming from the sky stimulates these three cones in particular proportions.

109
00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:36.930
The short-wavelength cones are strongly stimulated, but the medium- and long-wavelength cones are not left idle either, because the mixture also contains green and yellow light.

110
00:10:36.930 --> 00:10:44.100
The brain reads the ratio of these three signals, and the resulting perception is not violet, but a blue mixed with white.

111
00:10:44.100 --> 00:10:53.510
What's more, our eyes respond only weakly to violet light to begin with, and the lens of the eye yellows with age, filtering out even more of the short wavelengths.

112
00:10:53.510 --> 00:10:55.880
The violet is there.

113
00:10:55.880 --> 00:11:00.010
We simply lack the equipment to pick it out as a color of its own.

114
00:11:00.010 --> 00:11:07.980
In short, it would be wrong to say that "the sky is really violet and we simply see it wrong." The sky has no single true color.

115
00:11:07.980 --> 00:11:13.070
There is a spectrum of light, and there is a living creature that interprets that spectrum.

116
00:11:13.070 --> 00:11:19.600
A bee or a bird, with different cones in its eyes, may well perceive the same sky differently from us.

117
00:11:19.600 --> 00:11:23.890
This is the border where physics ends and biology begins.

118
00:11:23.890 --> 00:11:29.620
The blueness of the sky is made half in the atmosphere and half inside our own skulls.

119
00:11:29.620 --> 00:11:34.230
At the end of the day, the same physics paints a completely different scene.

120
00:11:34.230 --> 00:11:39.640
When the sun is high overhead, its light takes the shortest path through the atmosphere.

121
00:11:39.640 --> 00:11:44.410
As the sun nears the horizon, that path grows longer.

122
00:11:44.410 --> 00:11:51.260
When the sun sits right on the horizon line, its light passes through roughly thirty-eight times more air than it does at noon.

123
00:11:51.260 --> 00:11:57.150
On a journey that long, the blue light is scattered off in every direction before it is even halfway there.

124
00:11:57.150 --> 00:12:02.880
What reaches you are the longer wavelengths that survive the trip: yellow, orange and red.

125
00:12:02.880 --> 00:12:08.610
The crimson of a sunset is really the other side of the same scattering that makes the sky blue.

126
00:12:08.610 --> 00:12:12.900
The blue has been taken from your sun to become somebody else's sky.

127
00:12:12.900 --> 00:12:21.670
While you watch a red sun go down in the west, people thousands of kilometers to the east are living through their afternoon under that same scattered blue light.

128
00:12:21.670 --> 00:12:25.480
Larger particles in the air make the picture richer.

129
00:12:25.480 --> 00:12:30.570
Rayleigh's formula only holds for scatterers much smaller than the wavelength of light.

130
00:12:30.570 --> 00:12:42.220
For bigger particles, such as cloud droplets, a more general theory takes over, developed by the German physicist Gustav Mie in nineteen oh-eight, and these particles scatter all colors almost equally.

131
00:12:42.220 --> 00:12:48.430
That is why clouds look white, and why a hazy city sky looks pale and milky.

132
00:12:48.430 --> 00:12:57.840
The same principle explains why, after major volcanic eruptions, fine particles gathering high in the atmosphere can tint sunsets with unusual colors for months.

133
00:12:57.840 --> 00:13:02.290
But after sunset, the stage opens on a far quieter secret.

134
00:13:02.290 --> 00:13:09.460
Once the sun has slipped below the horizon, the sky overhead keeps glowing for a while in a deep, almost velvety blue.

135
00:13:09.460 --> 00:13:14.390
For a long time, this twilight blue was also put down to Rayleigh scattering.

136
00:13:14.390 --> 00:13:18.040
Yet the calculations did not add up.

137
00:13:18.040 --> 00:13:27.130
With the sun below the horizon, the light reaching the air overhead must have travelled a very long way through the atmosphere, and it should have lost most of its blue along the way.

138
00:13:27.130 --> 00:13:34.620
By a simple Rayleigh calculation, the twilight sky overhead should have looked greyish, or even faintly yellowish, rather than blue.

139
00:13:34.620 --> 00:13:39.550
The American physicist Edward Hulburt solved this puzzle in nineteen fifty-three.

140
00:13:39.550 --> 00:13:45.920
The key lay in the ozone layer, concentrated between fifteen and thirty-five kilometers above the ground.

141
00:13:45.920 --> 00:13:52.290
Ozone is known for absorbing ultraviolet light, but it also has a weak absorption band in visible light.

142
00:13:52.290 --> 00:13:59.620
This band, discovered by the French chemist James Chappuis in eighteen eighty, swallows mostly orange and yellow light.

143
00:13:59.620 --> 00:14:06.310
During the day the effect is barely noticeable, because sunlight crosses the ozone layer by a short route.

144
00:14:06.310 --> 00:14:12.360
At twilight, though, sunlight passes through the ozone layer sideways, for hundreds of kilometers.

145
00:14:12.360 --> 00:14:18.250
Along that long path the orange and yellow are filtered out, and the light that remains shifts toward blue.

146
00:14:18.250 --> 00:14:26.220
Hulburt's calculations showed that the deep blue of twilight comes mostly from this quiet filter of ozone, not from Rayleigh scattering.

147
00:14:26.220 --> 00:14:33.870
So the color you see above you as night falls is a signature of the very same layer of gas that shields you from ultraviolet rays.

148
00:14:33.870 --> 00:14:38.480
Perhaps the most beautiful test of this story took place on another planet.

149
00:14:38.480 --> 00:14:41.810
The atmosphere of Mars is very thin.

150
00:14:41.810 --> 00:14:48.500
The pressure at the surface is less than one percent of Earth's, and the air is made up mostly of carbon dioxide.

151
00:14:48.500 --> 00:14:52.470
In air this sparse, scattering by molecules stays weak.

152
00:14:52.470 --> 00:14:59.480
What gives the Martian sky its color is a fine, iron-oxide-rich dust that the winds keep constantly aloft.

153
00:14:59.480 --> 00:15:06.330
These dust grains are about one point five micrometers across, which makes them larger than the wavelength of visible light.

154
00:15:06.330 --> 00:15:12.380
The dust partly absorbs blue light, while scattering red and yellow tones in every direction.

155
00:15:12.380 --> 00:15:18.430
As a result, the daytime sky on Mars has a color somewhere between caramel and a pinkish brown.

156
00:15:18.430 --> 00:15:22.240
And at sunset, the colors trade places.

157
00:15:22.240 --> 00:15:30.370
Dust particles of this size are better at scattering blue light forward than red light, at angles very close to the direction the light is coming from.

158
00:15:30.370 --> 00:15:38.980
When the sun sinks toward the horizon and its light travels a long way through the dusty air, a cold, pale blue halo forms right around the sun.

159
00:15:38.980 --> 00:15:49.990
This sight had been known since the Viking missions, and on April fifteenth, twenty fifteen, the Curiosity rover showed it to the whole world in a series of photographs taken from Gale Crater.

160
00:15:49.990 --> 00:15:54.280
On Earth, the noon sky is blue and the evening is red.

161
00:15:54.280 --> 00:15:59.050
On Mars, the noon sky is red and the evening is blue.

162
00:15:59.050 --> 00:16:05.100
This reversal is the most elegant confirmation of an idea that is a hundred and fifty years old.

163
00:16:05.100 --> 00:16:14.350
The colour of the sky is not some magical property of the air; it is the result of the relationship between the wavelength of light and the size of the particles that scatter it,

164
00:16:14.350 --> 00:16:18.320
along with whatever absorbs light along the way.

165
00:16:18.320 --> 00:16:23.090
An astronomer who understands this calculation can now run it in reverse.

166
00:16:23.090 --> 00:16:28.980
By studying the light that filters through the atmospheres of planets passing in front of distant stars,

167
00:16:28.980 --> 00:16:36.310
astronomers are trying to tell whether those atmospheres are dominated by small molecules or by particles of haze and mist.

168
00:16:36.310 --> 00:16:46.360
Rayleigh's fourth-power curve shows up as a distinct slope in the spectrum of that light, and the shape of this slope offers a clue to what colour the sky might be on a world we have never seen.

169
00:16:46.360 --> 00:16:52.410
These estimates are not yet certain; they often rest on indirect measurements interpreted through models.

170
00:16:52.410 --> 00:16:57.020
But the principle is the same one at work in Tyndall's glass tube.

171
00:16:57.020 --> 00:17:04.190
The next time you look up at a clear midday sky, you might remember that what you are seeing is really the meeting of three separate worlds.

172
00:17:04.190 --> 00:17:15.040
There is light filtered by the atoms on the surface of the Sun, a wave scattered in every direction by the randomly vibrating molecules of the air, and three kinds of cells that read that wave as blue.

173
00:17:15.040 --> 00:17:22.210
Violet is there too, but the sky has kept it hidden, not for you, but perhaps for entirely different eyes.

174
00:17:22.210 --> 00:17:26.020
We have reached the end of today's episode of Nöron Bilim.

175
00:17:26.020 --> 00:17:28.870
Until we meet again in a new story.
